WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.874
In this part we meet two famous equations, Lagrange’s and Clairaut’s.

2
00:00:05.874 --> 00:00:12.514
Clairaut’s equation produces a family of straight lines and one special curve.

3
00:00:12.514 --> 00:00:20.006
You will also learn what must be checked before calling a candidate a singular solution.

4
00:00:20.256 --> 00:00:26.167
Lagrange’s equation: y equals x times phi of p plus psi of p.

5
00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:32.465
Differentiating gives a linear equation for x as a function of p.

6
00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:39.926
If phi of p equals p, we get Clairaut’s equation: y equals x p plus psi of p.

7
00:00:40.176 --> 00:00:45.762
Example: y equals x y prime minus y prime squared.

8
00:00:45.762 --> 00:00:52.465
Differentiate: p equals p plus x minus two p, times p prime.

9
00:00:52.465 --> 00:00:57.492
So x minus two p, times p prime, equals zero.

10
00:00:57.492 --> 00:00:59.726
There are two cases.

11
00:00:59.976 --> 00:01:03.812
Case one: p prime is zero, so p equals C.

12
00:01:03.812 --> 00:01:10.548
The general solution is a family of lines: y equals C x minus C squared.

13
00:01:10.548 --> 00:01:15.132
Case two: x equals two p, so p equals x over two.

14
00:01:15.132 --> 00:01:19.623
Substituting gives y equals x squared over four.

15
00:01:19.623 --> 00:01:22.430
This is the singular solution.

16
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:31.390
The graph shows the parabola y equals x squared over four and the lines for several values of C.

17
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.835
Each line touches the parabola at x equals two C.

18
00:01:35.835 --> 00:01:39.737
The parabola is the envelope of the family.

19
00:01:39.737 --> 00:01:47.630
Through each of its points pass two solutions with the same slope, so uniqueness fails.

20
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.997
A singular solution always lies on the p-discriminant curve,

21
00:01:52.997 --> 00:01:58.625
where F is zero and the derivative of F with respect to p is zero.

22
00:01:58.625 --> 00:02:00.842
But the converse is false!

23
00:02:00.842 --> 00:02:08.858
For y equals two x y prime minus y prime squared the discriminant curve is y equals x squared,

24
00:02:08.858 --> 00:02:11.501
which is not a solution at all.

25
00:02:11.501 --> 00:02:18.494
So always check: is it a solution, and does uniqueness fail at each of its points?

26
00:02:18.744 --> 00:02:24.518
Check yourself: which statement about the p-discriminant curve is true?
